00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.103
Actually, what I did want to
do is say just a little bit

00:01:57.103 --> 00:01:59.480
about the brief history
of convex optimization,

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.220
just to give some
historical background.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.760
So the first thing is that
as a mathematical field,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.370
it's at least 120 years old.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:11.690
So people were writing
down inequalities

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.720
that we would now say,
hey, that's convex,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:15.830
that's convexity.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.740
They were writing this down
in the late 19th century.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:23.450
That was codified right around
the turn of the last century.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:28.010
So well, I guess whatever, a
century and 1.2 centuries ago.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.007
So that was codified.

00:02:29.007 --> 00:02:30.590
And someone said,
look, we keep saying

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.173
the same stupid inequality all
the time, let's give it a name.

00:02:33.173 --> 00:02:38.540
And that's when the name
became basically convexity.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.150
So as a mathematical field,
it's really already quite well

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.860
developed in the '20s, '50s.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.830
By the '70s, I would
just say it was done.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:48.650
There was a beautiful book
written on convex analysis.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.600
If you do math and are
interested in math,

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:51.350
you could read it.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:52.370
It's a beautiful book.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:53.270
And that's just it.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:53.895
That's just it.

00:02:53.895 --> 00:02:56.780
It just-- have people
done stuff since then?

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:57.555
Yeah, sure.

00:02:57.555 --> 00:02:58.680
But basically, that was it.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.320
It was just cleanly wrapped up.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.700
It doesn't say anything about
what you do with it, how

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.308
do you apply it,
how do you actually

00:03:06.308 --> 00:03:08.600
do algorithmic things with
it, like how do you actually

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.460
solve these problems, but
just as a mathematical field,

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.710
it's super-- it's interesting.

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:15.522
And I'd even encourage you
to poke through that book

00:03:15.522 --> 00:03:21.260
if you have that proclivity,
the math proclivity.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.060
I would actually
suggest looking at it.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:28.340
OK, so when it really takes
off, and this is not--

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:33.060
this is roughly somewhere
around the 1940s.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.600
Now, this is--
it's not accidental

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.120
that coincides with the
development basically

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.920
of the computer.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.888
So because-- to me,
the exciting part

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:44.180
