00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:47.630
the ones used today
on nonconvex things

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:48.880
like training neural networks.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:53.530
OK, so there's your big picture.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:57.790
OK, so at this point,
unless there's questions,

00:06:57.790 --> 00:06:58.930
we're jumping in.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:00.250
And this is the deep end.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:03.550
So this is not, we're
not in the-- we're just

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.320
going to jump right in.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:09.330
OK, so the-- and
again, to remind you

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:12.930
what the whole point
of all this is is this

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.460
is not a class about math.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.890
It's a class about actually
applying these methods.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.390
And so when you
apply these methods,

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:22.920
you arrive at a startup or
an internship or something

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:26.940
like that or your research,
and there's a practical problem

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.090
you want to solve.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.910
That's the setting we're
talking about here.

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:36.060
And then you have to sort
through and try to figure out

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.640
if it's convex,
which parts of it

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.360
are convex, these
kinds of things.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.840
And so absolutely
critical is to be

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.793
able to recognize
things like a convex set

00:07:45.793 --> 00:07:46.710
and a convex function.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.270
We'll talk about
convex sets today,

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:50.010
convex functions next week.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:55.960
So that's the actual practical
skill that we are after.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:58.408
It is interesting theory, and
it's interesting as theory,

00:07:58.408 --> 00:07:59.200
and it's just fine.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.335
But the point is that's
the skill we're after.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:05.970
So what we'll do
is we're going to--

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.990
I'm going to start
with super basic,

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.160
and it's going to
accelerate after that.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:13.220
So let's start, go back
way to the beginning.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:15.970
So at the beginning,
it goes like this,

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:21.400
I just have two vectors, x1
and x2, and N dimensional space

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:22.790
or something like that.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:26.260
And what I do is, let's
suppose that they are not

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:27.380
equal to each other.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:30.280
And I'm going to form this
specific linear combination

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:31.030
here.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.940
So that linear combination has
got two coefficients, theta

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.080
and 1 minus theta.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:37.990
They're interesting
because they add up to 1.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.630
OK, so that's critical.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:45.280
Now, what geometrically,
what this is--

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:50.560
as you vary theta, this sketches
out the line that in this space

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:52.360
that passes through x1 and x2.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:53.590
So that's what that is.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:56.590
In fact, you would call this a
parameterization of the line.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:58.240
It's parameterized by theta.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.485
So theta equals 0.

00:08:59.485 --> 00:09:01.420
It means that you're at x2.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.490
Theta equals 1
means you're at x1.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:08.380
That equals 1/2, means you're at
the midpoint between x1 and x2.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.642
So this is the--
again, it's all just

00:09:10.642 --> 00:09:12.850
to go back to the beginning
and make sure everybody's

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.940
on the same page.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:20.090
So this is called an
affine combination.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.807
So a linear combination is--

00:09:22.807 --> 00:09:24.640
well, you know what a
linear combination is.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.590
It's a sum of vectors
with multiplied

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:28.600
by some coefficients.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:32.260
But when those coefficients
add up to 1, that's special,

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.840
and that's called an
affine combination.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.030
OK, so that's an
affine combination.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:40.030
So this is the simplest
affine combination

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:41.710
you could possibly have.

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:44.050
OK, all right.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:50.140
Now, an affine set is
then defined to be a set--

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:54.560
it contains the line through any
two distinct points in the set.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:55.915
In other words, it roughly--

00:09:55.915 --> 00:09:57.790
you might say informally
something like this,

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:00.530
it's closed under forming a
line or something like that.

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.530
So in other words, if two
points are in the set,

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:04.890
the entire line is in the set.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:07.050
So that's an affine set.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.390
And you'll see lots of examples.

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:12.930
But a famous one would
be this, the solution

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:14.530
is a set of linear inequalities.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.470
So the set of x
for which Ax equals

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:19.410
b is going to be an affine set.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.510
And by the way, you can check.

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:23.718
A lot of things I'm
going to say, honestly,

00:10:23.718 --> 00:10:25.260
you might want to
do something weird,

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:29.880
like just with probability 0.1,
audit that, and which means

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:30.940
try to show it.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.250
So in this case, what you'd
do is you'd have to say,

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:36.240
well, let's see if
this is an affine set.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.340
To do that, you would
say, well, let's suppose

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:42.840
x1 and x2 are both
solutions of Ax equals b,

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.400
so that's the first step.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.890
Then you'd say, let's let
theta be any number at all.

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:51.390
And then you'd form theta
x1 plus 1 minus theta x2.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:55.110
And you'd want to
show that point also

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:56.790
satisfies Ax equals b.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:58.000
It's quite straightforward.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.710
I'm not going to do it, but
I would recommend that you--

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:04.200
I'll say stuff, and you just
sit there and you go, cool.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.820
Yeah, it's probably
true if I say it.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:09.340
So you can trust me,
but I would actually

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.720
suggest that you audit it.

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:14.260
Like every 10th
thing you just say,

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:17.440
I'm going to show that because
I will say stuff without--

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:21.760
many, most of what I'm going to
say I'm not going to justify.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:24.310
OK, now it turns out this
is not just a good example.

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:25.150
It's generic.

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:28.300
So every affine set
is the solution set

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:30.160
of linear inequalities.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:33.100
And again, I'm not
going to show that.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:35.780
But this brings us
to a convex set.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:40.600
So if theta is in this
affine combination,

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:44.440
if theta is between
0 and 1, then this

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:48.460
traces out the line
segment between x1 and x2.

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:51.940
And by the way, some people
actually write it this way.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.530
And I'm totally OK with that.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:58.480
They write x1, x2 like
that, perfectly OK.

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:01.150
Now, we all know
and agree on what

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:04.210
that means if x1 and x2
are numbers, assuming,

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:07.060
in that case, it's conventional
for x2 to be bigger than x1.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:10.420
But nevertheless, we
know what this means.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:12.340
But this is for vectors.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.170
This would be the line segment.

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:16.720
This is not standard notation.

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:18.250
By the way, in
this case, this is

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:21.850
called either a convex
combination of x1 and x2.

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:24.140
But you're going to find
other names and other things.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.930
And some of them are
quite descriptive.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:26.530
Here's one.

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:28.520
It's a mixture.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:31.090
So it's just-- it's a
mixture of x1 and x2.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:32.360
It's everyone who gets it.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:34.570
And so then you'd say,
well, it's 22% of that

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:36.190
and 78% of that.

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:37.438
So it's a mixture.

00:12:37.438 --> 00:12:38.980
And there's other
names, too, and I'm

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.022
forgetting what they are.

00:12:40.022 --> 00:12:42.280
But in different fields,
you'd find different names

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.440
for a convex combination.

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:50.650
OK, so a convex set is one
that says any two-- if you have

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:53.320
any two points in the set,
the line segment between them

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:54.580
is in the set.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:58.750
Another way to say it is
any two points in the set

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:03.850
have a clear line of
sight to each other.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:05.710
That's what it says.

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:07.390
And so, here are some--

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:11.830
these are silly examples, but
here's a little convex set.

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:12.580
It's dark here.

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:14.200
That means it
contains its boundary.

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:15.760
And this is convex.

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:18.228
And we can check
because what you

00:13:18.228 --> 00:13:20.770
do is you'd say, well, look, if
I take any two points, here's

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:22.300
one, and here's the other.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:24.760
And then, I would simply
visualize the segment

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:25.510
in between them.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:27.100
Sure enough, it's
inside the set.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.440
It's all cool.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:31.780
This one is not convex.

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:35.350
And I have a counterexample
right here, which is,

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:36.520
here's a point in the set.

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:37.910
Here's a point in the set.

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:39.410
This is the line segment.

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:43.682
And you can see it goes
outside the set, so that set.

00:13:43.682 --> 00:13:47.000
That one, this one
is also not convex.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.470
But because it's weird, I
don't know if you can see,

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.420
but there are gaps
in the boundary here,

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:53.480
and I can have two points
on the boundary here.

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:56.480
They're both in the set, but
the line segment is not entirely

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:57.230
in the set.

00:13:57.230 --> 00:14:01.202
OK, so this is-- it's all--

00:14:01.202 --> 00:14:03.350
this is at the
center of the course.

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:05.000
So there's something--
that's going

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:08.030
to be something special
about convex sets.

00:14:08.030 --> 00:14:17.440
OK, now if you have
an affine combination

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.560
with all the
coefficients nonnegative,

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:23.090
so that that's going
to look like this--

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:26.800
so x is a linear
combination of x1 to xk.

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:27.952
These are vectors.

00:14:27.952 --> 00:14:29.410
Number one is a
linear combination.

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:31.210
Number two, the
coefficients add up to 1.

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:33.460
That makes it an
affine combination.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:35.680
And number three,
they're all nonnegative.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:37.510
That makes it a
convex combination.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:41.330
Otherwise, some people
call this a mixture.

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.193
Sometimes they say it's a
weighted sum or weighted--

00:14:44.193 --> 00:14:46.360
I've heard people say it's
a weighted average, which

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:47.260
is what it is.

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:49.600
It's an expectation
in probability

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:51.940
that if you take
the thetas to be

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:54.370
the probabilities of
a random vector that

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:56.995
takes the values
x1 through xk, then

00:14:56.995 --> 00:14:58.120
this is the expected value.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:14:59.680
That's the expected
value of whatever

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.900
these things, that
random variable.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:04.100
So that's a convex combination.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:07.510
And so the convex
hull is going--

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:11.840
that is actually-- it's a set.

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.510
It's the set of all
convex combinations

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:15.890
of points from that set.

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:18.890
So that's what the
convex hull is.

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:22.370
So here's an example over here.

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.160
So this is a finite set,
here at these points.

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:29.360
And then what's
drawn here in gray,

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:32.120
that's the convex
hull of these points.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:34.610
And you can check
if there's a point--

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:40.268
how do this is in
the convex hull?

00:15:40.268 --> 00:15:42.560
Well, that one was right on
the line between these two.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:46.670
But here's-- how do at this
point is in the convex hull?

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:49.610
Well, it's 1/3,
1/3, 1/3 of this.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:51.440
So that's how that got there.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:54.830
And so that's the picture.

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:58.730
Let me ask you a couple
of questions just for fun.

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:03.350
Are the coefficients-- that
point is in-- this point

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:05.480
is in the convex
hull right here.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:09.890
My question is, is there
just one set of thetas?

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:10.390
No.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:10.890
No.

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:16.300
No, that's not right because, in
fact, if it's in any triangle,

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:18.760
if it's in different
triangles, then

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:20.890
there can be different thetas.

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:23.200
So those coefficients
are not unique.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:24.940
So OK, all right.

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:28.570
So this is the convex hull.

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:31.315
OK, convex cone.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:37.420
So a conic or
nonnegative-- some people

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:40.300
call it a nonnegative
linear combination.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:41.590
That's another name for it.

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:45.098
So that's basically a linear
combination of things.

00:16:45.098 --> 00:16:47.140
But with the coefficients
have to be nonnegative.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:48.340
And, in fact, you might--

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:50.560
some people actually
say, like someone says,

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:52.030
what is convex analysis?

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:54.250
And here's one
pretty good answer.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:57.610
It's like linear algebra,
but we introduce asymmetries.

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:00.342
The coefficients
are nonnegative.

00:17:00.342 --> 00:17:02.550
In linear algebra, you don't
have these distinctions,

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:04.109
or you don't--

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:06.359
that's not a main point
of linear algebra.

00:17:06.359 --> 00:17:07.680
We have a linear combination.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:10.079
Here, we have a
nonnegative combination.

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:11.369
So that's one way to say it.

00:17:11.369 --> 00:17:13.605
It's like bringing
asymmetry to linear algebra.

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:21.180
So this would be, for example,
that's a conic combination

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:24.690
provided these two
numbers are nonnegative.

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:26.910
And if you want to
visualize what it does,

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:28.418
it's like something like this.

00:17:28.418 --> 00:17:30.210
It makes like this
little pie-shaped thing.

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.030
Here's 0, here's x1, here's x2.

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:38.610
And someone suggests, how do I
get this point in the conical?

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:40.620
Suggest a theta 1
and theta 2, please?

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:45.000
Right there, just approximately.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:47.600
I'm waiting.

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:50.452
Any suggestion?

00:17:50.452 --> 00:17:51.410
That point right there.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:52.160
1/2 and 1/5.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:53.240
What is it?

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:54.590
1/2 and 1/5.

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:56.270
1/2 and 1/5.

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.430
Yeah, OK, I trust you.

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.290
That sounds good to me.

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:03.770
I was going to go like 1/5, 1/5,
but no, you're-- that's fine.

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:04.580
That's good enough.

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:06.560
Yeah, so that's it.

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:09.860
So that's what a convex cone is.

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:14.120
So again, none of
what we're saying

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:15.930
is in any practical context.

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:20.240
So you're like-- a perfectly
reasonable question

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:22.160
is, who cares?

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:23.370
We will get to that.

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:24.440
So there is a question.

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:28.470
The last slide-- is the
right-most picture also a hull?

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:28.970
And it's--

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:29.420
Yes.

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:30.462
--to combine the example.

00:18:30.462 --> 00:18:33.166
Yes, so I will show
you what that is.

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:41.450
This thing here is
not a convex set.

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:43.370
It's a nonconvex
set, this thing.

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:47.820
And the convex hull
consists of all--

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:50.190
anything that is a
convex combination

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:51.730
of any two points in that set.

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:54.370
So this is already in it.

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:57.390
But what's going to
happen is here this,

00:18:57.390 --> 00:18:59.550
it says that everything
on this line segment

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:02.460
is in the convex hull, but I
can move these points around.

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:06.250
And basically, I can even take
a point there and a point there,

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:09.270
and then everything
along there would also

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:10.420
be in the convex hull.

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:13.500
So if you look carefully
at the next one,

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:15.990
you'll see that
that's the convex hull

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.390
of the original set.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:21.870
So that make sense?

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:22.530
Yeah.

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:25.950
So the convex hull is
like the smallest convex

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:27.990
set that contains a set?

00:19:27.990 --> 00:19:30.240
That is precisely correct.

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:30.810
That's right.

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:35.880
So the convex hull of a set
is the smallest convex set

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:37.710
that contains a set.

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:39.510
That's right.

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:41.580
And weirdly, it has--
these things actually

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.950
have a lot of practical
applications, which

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:45.025
we'll get to later.

00:19:45.025 --> 00:19:45.525
Yeah.

00:19:45.525 --> 00:19:47.310
How does the picture
for the convex cone

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:50.530
have the zero point
but not the other sets?

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:54.240
A great question.

00:19:54.240 --> 00:20:00.000
So actually-- so here, if we
go to a conic combination,

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.710
the thetas could be--

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.420
here's a valid choice
of theta ready.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:07.050
Theta 1 equals 0, theta 2
equals zero, then I get 0.

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:14.190
So 0 is always a
conic combination

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:16.410
of any set of vectors
an except empty set.

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:18.120
But that's silly.

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:21.600
So that's-- you're not
allowed to do that for convex.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:23.640
The coefficients
have to add up to 1.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:25.410
And you can't say 00.

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:28.320
You can say 0, 1,
you can say 1/3, 2/3,

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.030
but you cannot say 00.

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:31.440
Does it make sense?

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:32.580
OK, cool.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:34.530
Yes.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:38.640
Does the convex hull necessarily
contain its boundary?

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:40.658
Does it necessarily contain a--

00:20:40.658 --> 00:20:41.700
--contain its boundaries?

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:44.110
--contain its boundary.

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:46.740
It doesn't necessarily, yeah.

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:52.230
So oh, also, maybe
I'll mention something.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:56.370
You're welcome to-- so you're
getting a little bit close

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:58.870
to analysis, which is fine.

00:20:58.870 --> 00:21:00.870
I think it's great.

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:04.020
I was trained in math.

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:07.680
But we're not going to focus
in this class on the analysis.

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:11.490
And by the way, questions
like that, by all means, ask.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:14.370
But if you start asking very
esoteric, weird questions

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:19.020
that only people who speak
the dialect of analysis know,

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:22.230
I'll just say, we'll
talk about it later.

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:25.990
So this was below that
threshold and a great question,

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:27.360
but in general.

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:28.530
And you can do that.

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:31.230
So you can-- if you actually
are interested in the math

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:33.750
and you want to know about
does this work if that's

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:35.820
closed and blah,
blah, blah, and what

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:37.650
limits of that and all
that kind of stuff,

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:41.400
you would speak
to the TAs or me.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:43.920
But we'll only do it in--
we won't do it in public,

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:45.978
though, because you're
not-- you shouldn't, you

00:21:45.978 --> 00:21:47.520
shouldn't talk about
detailed-- well,

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:49.978
you're just not supposed to
speak a weird dialect in public

00:21:49.978 --> 00:21:52.290
when no one understands.

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:54.510
If you don't know
analysis, you're

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:57.090
actually going to be
just fine in the class.

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:59.790
So you'll be just fine.

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:01.560
So there'll be some terms.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:05.250
We will give you the colloquial
translation of the term,

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:07.887
and you will be just fine.

00:22:07.887 --> 00:22:08.970
So don't worry about that.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:11.100
You might-- by
the way, if you're

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:13.830
doing a PhD in a field
where people use math,

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:15.870
even incidentally,
I would strongly

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:18.450
recommend you do take analysis.

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:19.780
Shall I explain why?

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:20.280
Yeah.

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:21.120
So here's why.

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:23.820
You might ask, oh, will
I use the material?

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:26.610
No, you'll never use it.

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.560
It's super interesting.

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:33.240
But the thing about it, you
take analysis for mostly,

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:34.830
at least for some set of people.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:38.526
If you're in math, you take
it because you have to.

00:22:38.526 --> 00:22:41.760
Well, you want to, I should
say, or something's wrong.

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:44.910
So you take it for
intellectual self-defense.

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:46.840
That's why you take it.

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:49.510
If you don't take it--
well, let's suppose you do--

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:52.640
suppose you do a PhD in
statistics or something,

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:55.090
something that's math adjacent.

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:58.210
So you're at a conference
someday five years from now,

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:00.190
and you give a
perfectly good paper.

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:03.280
Everything that you said
is right, it's interesting.

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:06.430
Some jerk in the
back raises the hand

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:12.160
and says, oh, that is
that week star compact?

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:14.410
And if you've had analysis
and you look at it,

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:19.240
and you go number one, yes,
number two, totally irrelevant.

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:21.040
If you haven't had
analysis, you'll

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:24.090
go like, oh, oh, I don't know.

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:25.850
And you'll have a
crisis and all that.

00:23:25.850 --> 00:23:27.370
So I'm just saying
that's why it's

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:29.050
worth a 10-week
investment, taking

00:23:29.050 --> 00:23:32.170
analysis for
math-adjacent courses,

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:33.940
like suppose you do robotics.

00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:37.550
And by the way, there are
these people out there.

00:23:37.550 --> 00:23:38.870
I know them.

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:41.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:43.640
The other thing-- OK,
on that same topic,

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:44.960
another one is this.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:50.900
For people who do
analysis, some of them

