And that would be a so-called
L-infinity norm ball.

00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:13.000
OK, that makes sense.

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:15.030
So that's the--

00:36:15.030 --> 00:36:19.230
OK, now we can also look
at things like norm cones.

00:36:19.230 --> 00:36:20.680
This is super interesting.

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:23.280
It's actually going to
be a theme in the class,

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:24.930
is that we're going
to be looking--

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:26.305
a lot of times
when we're looking

00:36:26.305 --> 00:36:32.730
at sets of in dimension n,
we'll actually look at RN plus 1

00:36:32.730 --> 00:36:34.300
where there's an
additional thing.

00:36:34.300 --> 00:36:35.400
And, in fact,
you're used to this

00:36:35.400 --> 00:36:36.480
because this is
like when you talk

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:38.050
about the graph of a function.

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:39.930
That's what you're doing.

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:43.260
If you have a function on
R2, you think of its graph.

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:45.570
Well, its graph is
something that lives in R3,

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:50.400
and it consists of points that
look like this, x1, x2 comma,

00:36:50.400 --> 00:36:53.327
F of x1x2, and that's the graph.

00:36:53.327 --> 00:36:54.910
So this is going to
be a common thing.

00:36:54.910 --> 00:36:58.170
So here, the set of
xt, t is a scalar,

00:36:58.170 --> 00:37:01.320
x is a vector for which the
norm of x is less than t.

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:03.750
That's actually a norm cone.

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:06.120
By the way, you
have to show that.

00:37:06.120 --> 00:37:11.610
If it's a cone, it says I can
multiply the pair xt by 7,

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:13.590
for example, and it's
still in the cone.

00:37:13.590 --> 00:37:16.470
And that would be true here
because if I multiply x by 7,

00:37:16.470 --> 00:37:18.540
the norm goes up by
7, but so does t.

00:37:18.540 --> 00:37:20.400
So everything's cool.

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:23.160
And then an example would be--

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:28.110
this is what people call
the Euclidean norm cone.

00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:30.190
It's got all sorts
of other names.

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:32.400
It's also called the
second-order cone.

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:33.840
That's a good way to say it.

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:36.210
Other names are--
here's a cool one.

00:37:36.210 --> 00:37:39.660
It's the Lorentzian cone
because it's the inequality that

00:37:39.660 --> 00:37:41.330
comes up in special relativity.

00:37:41.330 --> 00:37:43.080
Although, actually,
to be honest with you,

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:46.170
the Lorentzian cone has actually
got the bottom part, too,

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:48.000
which we don't do.

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:51.210
It's half of a Lorentzian
cone, or people would just

00:37:51.210 --> 00:37:53.250
call that a Lorentzian cone.

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:56.790
And then somebody
at some point tried

00:37:56.790 --> 00:38:01.620
to propagate people referring
to this as an ice cream cone,

00:38:01.620 --> 00:38:05.130
and I am extremely happy
to report it did not take.

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:10.020
So that's another
name for this cone.

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:11.700
OK, so these are--
and this just--

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:15.730
we're just going over here
are some common convex sets.

00:38:15.730 --> 00:38:19.820
Next up, polyhedra
and also polytopes.

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:21.573
I'll say something about that.

00:38:21.573 --> 00:38:23.240
So here's some notation
that we're going

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:25.830
to use throughout the class.

00:38:25.830 --> 00:38:29.340
So that's a vector Ax,
and that's a vector.

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:33.170
So I have a weird squiggly
inequality between vectors.

00:38:33.170 --> 00:38:35.690
And what we're
going to-- this is

00:38:35.690 --> 00:38:38.660
going to be element-wise
inequality between vectors.

00:38:38.660 --> 00:38:40.580
It means that every
entry of one vector

00:38:40.580 --> 00:38:42.830
is less than or equal to
every entry of the other one.

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:44.480
That's what it means.

00:38:44.480 --> 00:38:48.410
So Ax less than b, if you
think of it row by row,

00:38:48.410 --> 00:38:53.300
each row is a single
linear inequality.

00:38:53.300 --> 00:38:55.410
So that's one way
to think of it.

00:38:55.410 --> 00:39:03.830
And in fact, here these ais
transpose are the rows of A.

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:08.040
So basically, Ax less
than b says, well,

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:10.880
a1 transpose x has
to be less than b1,

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:12.230
but that's a halfspace.

00:39:12.230 --> 00:39:16.640
That's everybody who lives on
this side of this hyperplane,

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:18.890
and that's the outward normal.

00:39:18.890 --> 00:39:23.580
But also, a2 transpose x
has to be less than b2,

00:39:23.580 --> 00:39:26.040
and that's everybody
that lives on this side.

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:27.640
But we have to
satisfy all of those.

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:29.700
So that's basically
taking the intersection

00:39:29.700 --> 00:39:33.030
of these halfspaces, and we
get a set that looks like that.

00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:35.700
And that's called a polyhedron.

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:38.430
So it's literally
the solution of a set

00:39:38.430 --> 00:39:40.560
of linear inequalities.

00:39:40.560 --> 00:39:42.120
OK, that's what a polyhedron is.

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:48.060
And there's several-- some
people call these polytopes.

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:51.240
Then, to make it more
confusing, some authors--

00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:54.180
and actually weirdly, it's
reversed and not consistent.

00:39:54.180 --> 00:39:57.450
Some authors say that if
a polyhedron is bounded,

00:39:57.450 --> 00:39:58.980
it's a polytope.

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:02.878
And some have the exact
opposite convention.

00:40:02.878 --> 00:40:05.170
And it turns out for us, it's
not even going to matter.

00:40:05.170 --> 00:40:08.580
So just for us, they're
going to be the same.

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:11.610
But just to warn you, you might
be reading something somewhere,

00:40:11.610 --> 00:40:14.760
and that author might
have in their mind

00:40:14.760 --> 00:40:19.170
some idea that one of these
two is bounded and whatever.

00:40:19.170 --> 00:40:23.170
And these are convex.

00:40:23.170 --> 00:40:26.980
OK, here's an interesting cone.

00:40:26.980 --> 00:40:29.330
It's going to come up a lot.

00:40:29.330 --> 00:40:31.570
So I already mentioned this.

00:40:31.570 --> 00:40:34.450
Sn is a set of symmetric
N by N matrices.

00:40:34.450 --> 00:40:36.325
By the way, what's the
dimension of that set?

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:42.490
We've got N squared
numbers in it.

00:40:42.490 --> 00:40:46.210
So what's the dimension?

00:40:46.210 --> 00:40:47.800
What's that?

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:49.150
[INAUDIBLE]

00:40:49.150 --> 00:40:51.100
Yeah, it's N times N minus--

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:52.240
N plus 1.

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:55.540
It's N times n plus
1 divided by 2.

00:40:55.540 --> 00:40:58.600
And the reason is there's
N squared numbers,

00:40:58.600 --> 00:41:00.680
but the upper 1/2 is the
same as the bottom half,

00:41:00.680 --> 00:41:02.500
so it's redundant.

00:41:02.500 --> 00:41:06.400
So that's a vector space of
dimension, n plus 1 over 2.

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:10.008
B when we put a plus
there, it's the set

00:41:10.008 --> 00:41:11.800
of things which are
positive semi-definite.

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:13.885
Now, here we are basically--

00:41:13.885 --> 00:41:15.010
we're doing something here.

00:41:15.010 --> 00:41:19.420
We are overloading the
squiggly inequality symbol.

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:21.730
So you saw it on
the previous slide

00:41:21.730 --> 00:41:23.380
between vectors,
in which case, it

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:25.910
means element-wise inequality.

00:41:25.910 --> 00:41:29.690
Now, it appears between
symmetric matrices.

00:41:29.690 --> 00:41:32.288
And here, it means it's
positive semidefinite.

00:41:32.288 --> 00:41:34.580
The difference is-- actually,
we'll get into that a bit

00:41:34.580 --> 00:41:36.530
later today, but
that's the picture.

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:39.680
So that is called polymorphism?

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:43.310
Or what's it called when
the semantics of a symbol

00:41:43.310 --> 00:41:47.180
depends on the
syntactic context?

00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:48.590
Or is that just overloading?

00:41:48.590 --> 00:41:50.570
It doesn't matter.

00:41:50.570 --> 00:41:51.680
Somebody knows here.

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:52.182
Come on.

00:41:52.182 --> 00:41:54.140
There has got to be some
CS people here anyway.

00:41:54.140 --> 00:41:58.310
So fine, OK, that's the idea.

00:41:58.310 --> 00:42:00.080
OK, so here's an example.

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:02.040
Here's a quick example, is--

00:42:02.040 --> 00:42:06.840
this is silly, but here's a 2 by
2 matrix, and we want to know--

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:08.780
and so a general 2
by 2 symmetric matrix

00:42:08.780 --> 00:42:12.555
looks like xy yz because
this has got to equal that.

00:42:12.555 --> 00:42:14.930
And the question is, when is
that positive semi-definite?

00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:16.370
And you know the answer.

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:18.860
The answer is this has
got to be nonnegative.

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:20.480
That's got to be nonnegative.

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:23.150
And then the determinant
has to be positive.

00:42:23.150 --> 00:42:28.320
And that says that xz is
bigger than y squared.

00:42:28.320 --> 00:42:31.800
So that's the last inequality.

00:42:31.800 --> 00:42:37.110
And then here we've
drawn it for xy and z.

00:42:37.110 --> 00:42:38.960
And what it is,
it's the interior

00:42:38.960 --> 00:42:40.710
of this, which we don't
show or something,

00:42:40.710 --> 00:42:43.563
but that's the boundary of it.

00:42:43.563 --> 00:42:45.480
Actually, do you want
to know something weird?

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:48.040
If you twist your head
just the right way,

00:42:48.040 --> 00:42:51.660
it turns out that it is exactly
the same as a second-order

00:42:51.660 --> 00:42:53.670
cone, but kind of just rotated--

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:55.030
but it doesn't matter.

00:42:55.030 --> 00:42:55.950
This is what it is.

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:01.830
And, look, positive
semidefinite matrices

00:43:01.830 --> 00:43:04.680
are going to come up in many
fields that I hope many of you

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:05.745
have seen.

00:43:05.745 --> 00:43:07.980
The obvious one is
they are covariance

00:43:07.980 --> 00:43:09.990
matrices just to start.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:11.910
They describe ellipsoids.

00:43:11.910 --> 00:43:13.650
That's if you do stuff
that's geometrical

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:15.150
and you want to
describe ellipsoids.

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:17.077
They're parametrized
by, at some point,

00:43:17.077 --> 00:43:18.660
in various different
ways, but they're

00:43:18.660 --> 00:43:20.730
parametrized by positive
semidefinate matrices.

00:43:20.730 --> 00:43:22.350
So positive
semidefinite matrices

00:43:22.350 --> 00:43:24.840
come up in a ton of
different fields.

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:29.580
OK, so that's the
so-called PSD cone.

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:33.130
That's what people would say.

00:43:33.130 --> 00:43:35.950
So we could go on, and I
could show you all sorts

00:43:35.950 --> 00:43:37.990
of entertaining convex sets.

00:43:37.990 --> 00:43:40.960
But in fact, now we're going to
get to how this actually really

00:43:40.960 --> 00:43:41.840
is going to work.

00:43:41.840 --> 00:43:44.530
So the way it's
really going to work

00:43:44.530 --> 00:43:51.370
is we're actually going to
do this using a calculus.

00:43:51.370 --> 00:43:54.130
And so, let's stop and talk
about what calculus is.

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:59.050
So calculus is
outmoded and silly,

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:01.300
and I can't imagine that
they force people to take it,

00:44:01.300 --> 00:44:01.960
but they still do.

00:44:01.960 --> 00:44:02.470
It's fine.

00:44:02.470 --> 00:44:06.388
But what it is, if
you think about it,

00:44:06.388 --> 00:44:07.930
it's actually a
really good way to be

00:44:07.930 --> 00:44:10.870
able to calculate derivatives
of things, for example.

00:44:10.870 --> 00:44:13.240
And so I tell you
about some basic things

00:44:13.240 --> 00:44:15.940
like powers, and sine, and log.

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:19.330
And then I tell you some
rules like the derivative

00:44:19.330 --> 00:44:22.220
of a product, or I don't know.

00:44:22.220 --> 00:44:23.137
It's fancy rules, too.

00:44:23.137 --> 00:44:23.887
It doesn't matter.

00:44:23.887 --> 00:44:25.510
So I tell you these
rules, and then

00:44:25.510 --> 00:44:27.430
what you do is you
take your basic things,

00:44:27.430 --> 00:44:29.980
which we're going to call
atoms, which you know,

00:44:29.980 --> 00:44:33.310
like, oh, the derivative
of sine is cosine.

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:36.550
And then, you put it-- and
then, a couple of rules.

00:44:36.550 --> 00:44:40.600
And with a couple of rules
and knowing 30, 40 atoms,

00:44:40.600 --> 00:44:42.410
you're in good shape.

00:44:42.410 --> 00:44:43.555
You can actually calculate.

00:44:43.555 --> 00:44:45.430
You know the chain rule,
and you can actually

00:44:45.430 --> 00:44:50.260
calculate the gradient of some
logistic-- multinomial logistic

00:44:50.260 --> 00:44:52.360
loss or something like that.

00:44:52.360 --> 00:44:55.360
So we're going to do, actually,
something extremely similar

00:44:55.360 --> 00:44:57.490
in spirit, identical.

00:44:57.490 --> 00:45:01.600
So we are channeling the 17th
century here or maybe 18th

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:04.030
or something, to be fair.

00:45:04.030 --> 00:45:07.450
And so you've seen
some basic convex sets.

00:45:07.450 --> 00:45:10.960
And then we're going to
look at some methods that

00:45:10.960 --> 00:45:12.550
preserve convexity.

00:45:12.550 --> 00:45:15.490
And then you will show.

00:45:15.490 --> 00:45:18.280
And a very effective method
to know that a set is convex

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:20.805
is to actually construct
it using these rules.

00:45:20.805 --> 00:45:22.930
So another way to say that
is we're going to bring,

00:45:22.930 --> 00:45:28.420
we're going to have syntactic
verification of convexity

00:45:28.420 --> 00:45:30.340
of a set.

00:45:30.340 --> 00:45:33.802
So what it means is you
start by saying, well,

00:45:33.802 --> 00:45:35.510
that's an ellipsoid,
that's an ellipsoid,

00:45:35.510 --> 00:45:38.030
those are convex, that's a
halfspace, that's a polyhedron.

00:45:38.030 --> 00:45:40.250
Then I intersect them
or something, that's

00:45:40.250 --> 00:45:43.250
convex and stuff like that.

00:45:43.250 --> 00:45:46.370
But let's back up because
that's the correct way to do it.

00:45:46.370 --> 00:45:48.855
But I'll say something
about other ways.

00:45:48.855 --> 00:45:50.980
If a person walks up to
you on the street and says,

00:45:50.980 --> 00:45:54.780
is this set convex or not--

00:45:54.780 --> 00:45:56.713
that happens, by the way.

00:45:56.713 --> 00:45:58.880
That actually did happen
to me once, which is weird.

00:45:58.880 --> 00:46:00.710
But I was in New York.

00:46:00.710 --> 00:46:01.460
It was very weird.

00:46:01.460 --> 00:46:03.510
OK, anyway, it
actually happened.

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:05.780
So OK, fine.

00:46:05.780 --> 00:46:10.710
I think it happened because
they'd seen me talk about this,

00:46:10.710 --> 00:46:12.470
and then they thought,
well, hey, there he

00:46:12.470 --> 00:46:13.595
is, I'm going to go anyway.

00:46:13.595 --> 00:46:15.020
Fine.

00:46:15.020 --> 00:46:18.860
OK, so how do you
show a set is convex?

00:46:18.860 --> 00:46:24.210
Well, OK, so one way is just
to roll up your sleeves and go,

00:46:24.210 --> 00:46:26.810
OK, let x1 be any
point in the set,

00:46:26.810 --> 00:46:30.470
let x2 be any point in the set,
let theta be between 0 and 1.

00:46:30.470 --> 00:46:34.940
And then, just argue that this
point, this mixture or convex

00:46:34.940 --> 00:46:37.250
combination of x1,
x2 is in the set.

00:46:37.250 --> 00:46:42.420
Now, that's the right
thing to do almost never.

00:46:42.420 --> 00:46:44.720
Every now and then, you may
have to fall back to that,

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:48.200
but it should be the
absolute last resort.

00:46:48.200 --> 00:46:50.270
So you don't want to do that.

00:46:53.030 --> 00:46:55.490
Oh, well, let me tell you--

00:46:55.490 --> 00:46:57.410
so the way we're going
to do it is actually

00:46:57.410 --> 00:47:00.733
going to be constructive or
syntactic or by a calculus.

00:47:00.733 --> 00:47:02.150
And the way that's
going to happen

00:47:02.150 --> 00:47:05.210
is what we're going to do is
we're going to take a set C,

00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:08.960
and we're going to represent
it as a bunch of operations

00:47:08.960 --> 00:47:13.087
applied to sets already
known to be convex,

00:47:13.087 --> 00:47:15.170
a small number, the ones
you've just seen already,

00:47:15.170 --> 00:47:17.810
half spaces, polyhedra,
norm balls, norm cones.

00:47:17.810 --> 00:47:18.890
That's it.

00:47:18.890 --> 00:47:20.310
That's what we're going to do.

00:47:20.310 --> 00:47:22.490
Now, some of these
things are obvious,

00:47:22.490 --> 00:47:24.530
like intersection
affine functions.

00:47:24.530 --> 00:47:26.960
We're going-- you will see
later today some things that

00:47:26.960 --> 00:47:30.920
are not obvious at all.

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:34.190
So there's something else
I didn't put on here.

00:47:34.190 --> 00:47:35.810
It's actually a method--

00:47:35.810 --> 00:47:38.335
it could be method
3 or method 0.

00:47:38.335 --> 00:47:40.460
I didn't want it on the
slides for the reasons that

00:47:40.460 --> 00:47:42.390
will become clear when
I tell you what it is.

00:47:42.390 --> 00:47:43.830
So here's another method.

00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:45.830
Here's a street fighting
method for figuring out

00:47:45.830 --> 00:47:47.570
if something is convex.

00:47:47.570 --> 00:47:49.550
What you do is you write--

00:47:49.550 --> 00:47:53.300
I'm assuming you have some
code that will tell you

00:47:53.300 --> 00:47:57.080
if a point is in the set
C. Let's assume that.

00:47:57.080 --> 00:47:58.190
Then here's what you do.

00:47:58.190 --> 00:48:00.320
Also, you have some
way of generating,

00:48:00.320 --> 00:48:02.990
let's say, random, but
just points inside C.

00:48:02.990 --> 00:48:04.310
So here's what you do.

00:48:04.310 --> 00:48:05.930
You write a code that does this.

00:48:05.930 --> 00:48:07.760
It generates two
points inside C.

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:11.930
It picks a theta at random
between 0 and 1, forms

00:48:11.930 --> 00:48:13.400
this thing, and
then checks if it's

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:17.870
in C. Everybody got my method?

00:48:17.870 --> 00:48:19.830
Now you go to lunch.

00:48:19.830 --> 00:48:21.270
Everybody, this is critical.

00:48:21.270 --> 00:48:22.853
This is a very
important part of this.

00:48:22.853 --> 00:48:24.050
You go to lunch now.

00:48:24.050 --> 00:48:26.420
And so and you come back--

00:48:26.420 --> 00:48:28.790
and by the way, you make
it halt the first time

00:48:28.790 --> 00:48:31.760
it finds a violation.

00:48:31.760 --> 00:48:34.580
Everybody? --in
which case, for sure,

00:48:34.580 --> 00:48:38.210
it's not convex because it
just produced an x1 and x2

00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:39.750
and a theta between 0 and 1.

00:48:39.750 --> 00:48:42.818
And the convex combination is
not x1 and x2 and C. Convex

00:48:42.818 --> 00:48:43.610
combination is not.

00:48:43.610 --> 00:48:44.402
Everybody got that?

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:50.600
--in which case, you
immediately remove all evidence

00:48:50.600 --> 00:48:53.780
that you ever did this.

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:56.300
You remove the script.

00:48:56.300 --> 00:48:58.970
So just, it's gone.

00:48:58.970 --> 00:49:01.610
Don't push it or
anything to GitHub.

00:49:01.610 --> 00:49:03.110
It's just gone.

00:49:03.110 --> 00:49:06.500
Then, what happens is
when you're hanging out

00:49:06.500 --> 00:49:09.050
with your friends or something,
somebody says, I don't know,

00:49:09.050 --> 00:49:10.970
hey, do you think
the set of measures

00:49:10.970 --> 00:49:12.630
that blah, blah, blah, this
that, and the other thing

00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:13.713
is some complicated thing?

00:49:13.713 --> 00:49:16.220
And you'd go like, of
course, that's not convex.

00:49:16.220 --> 00:49:18.663
That's what-- and
they go, really?

00:49:18.663 --> 00:49:19.580
How did you know that?

00:49:19.580 --> 00:49:22.190
And you go like, intuition.

00:49:22.190 --> 00:49:22.730
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:22.730 --> 00:49:23.900
So that's what you do.

00:49:23.900 --> 00:49:25.140
Everybody following this?

00:49:25.140 --> 00:49:25.640
Yeah.

00:49:25.640 --> 00:49:26.570
OK.

00:49:26.570 --> 00:49:28.640
Now, suppose you
come back from lunch,

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:32.900
and it's looked at 100 million
different pairs x1 and x2,

00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:35.720
and it has not yet found a
convex combination is not

00:49:35.720 --> 00:49:36.320
in the set.

00:49:36.320 --> 00:49:37.460
Everybody following this?

00:49:37.460 --> 00:49:39.560
What precisely do at that time?

00:49:42.260 --> 00:49:42.760
Nothing.

00:49:42.760 --> 00:49:45.790
You know nothing whatsoever.

00:49:45.790 --> 00:49:48.310
By the way, this is still
fine because now-- then you

00:49:48.310 --> 00:49:49.960
can try to show it.

00:49:49.960 --> 00:49:52.270
And then, in your next paper,
you can say, well, look,

00:49:52.270 --> 00:49:53.870
this is convex.

00:49:53.870 --> 00:49:56.380
It's not totally obvious, but
here's a paragraph derivation

00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:57.910
where you could do that.

00:49:57.910 --> 00:50:01.270
But if you fail, you still win.

00:50:01.270 --> 00:50:04.510
You go to the next conference,
you hang out with your friends,

00:50:04.510 --> 00:50:08.125
and they say things like wow,
is the set of blah, blah, blah,

00:50:08.125 --> 00:50:09.250
do you think that's convex?

00:50:09.250 --> 00:50:11.680
And you'd go, I think--

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:12.790
I guess so.

00:50:12.790 --> 00:50:16.120
And they would say, how do
you-- how could you guess that?

00:50:16.120 --> 00:50:18.640
And you'd say intuition.

00:50:18.640 --> 00:50:19.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:19.870 --> 00:50:22.420
And then usually if you'll--
what will happen is two months

00:50:22.420 --> 00:50:26.650
later somebody, I don't know,
maybe a Russian grad student

00:50:26.650 --> 00:50:29.260
at the conference or something,
will post something on archive

00:50:29.260 --> 00:50:30.490
showing that it isn't--

00:50:30.490 --> 00:50:34.660
in which case you'll say, I
knew that, which is a lie.

00:50:34.660 --> 00:50:35.680
But OK, so fine.

00:50:35.680 --> 00:50:36.850
All right.

00:50:36.850 --> 00:50:38.500
So that's off the record.

00:50:38.500 --> 00:50:43.745
That was your method, that's--
but it's actually not at all

00:50:43.745 --> 00:50:45.020
a bad thing to do.

00:50:45.020 --> 00:50:46.820
I would not judge some--

00:50:46.820 --> 00:50:49.580
I would if it was something
where, in three lines,

00:50:49.580 --> 00:50:50.900
you could see it's convex.

00:50:50.900 --> 00:50:53.270
But if it's not obvious,
I would totally--

00:50:53.270 --> 00:50:56.280
I personally, I'll go ahead and
admit it right here in public,

00:50:56.280 --> 00:50:58.970
I've done that.

00:50:58.970 --> 00:51:02.940
And then I go right to my
Russian friends and say,

00:51:02.940 --> 00:51:04.580
is this convex?

00:51:04.580 --> 00:51:08.840
And then we have a notion if I
send an email to Yuri Nesterov,

00:51:08.840 --> 00:51:09.950
"does it delay?"

00:51:09.950 --> 00:51:12.650
and then three days later,
he says, I am not sure.

00:51:12.650 --> 00:51:15.410
That's the equivalent
of it's unknown.

00:51:15.410 --> 00:51:18.320
It's probably the equivalent
of it's unknowable, actually.

00:51:18.320 --> 00:51:20.570
So anyway, that's how I do it.

00:51:20.570 --> 00:51:22.580
But anyway, so that's

00:51:22.580 --> 00:51:23.910
OK, fine.

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:25.610
Let's move on.

00:51:25.610 --> 00:51:29.810
OK, so we have the idea
of an intersection.

00:51:29.810 --> 00:51:32.630
So this is obvious.

00:51:32.630 --> 00:51:36.350
This is geometrically
very obvious.

00:51:36.350 --> 00:51:39.770
If I have-- here's a convex set.

00:51:39.770 --> 00:51:43.270
And here's another one.

00:51:43.270 --> 00:51:45.370
And the intersection
is this thing.

00:51:45.370 --> 00:51:47.710
And look, your eyeball
tells you it's convex.

00:51:47.710 --> 00:51:49.630
And by the way, a
three-line proof

00:51:49.630 --> 00:51:51.310
would tell you that
it's convex, too.

00:51:51.310 --> 00:51:54.700
OK, so the intersection
of convex sets is convex.

00:51:54.700 --> 00:51:57.920
Actually, the intersection
can be even an infinite number

00:51:57.920 --> 00:52:02.140
of things can be-- so we can
talk about things an infinite

00:52:02.140 --> 00:52:02.650
number--

00:52:02.650 --> 00:52:05.990
the intersection of an
infinite number of half spaces

00:52:05.990 --> 00:52:08.450
is convex.

00:52:08.450 --> 00:52:10.985
So here's an example.

00:52:10.985 --> 00:52:13.230
Yeah, it's quasi-practical,
or something.

00:52:13.230 --> 00:52:14.280
It goes like this.

00:52:14.280 --> 00:52:16.550
So here's my set S.

00:52:16.550 --> 00:52:20.330
This is called a
trigonometric polynomial.

00:52:20.330 --> 00:52:23.720
So it comes up in lots of fields
like in signal processing,

00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:24.800
for example, or whatever.

00:52:24.800 --> 00:52:25.610
But there it is.

00:52:25.610 --> 00:52:29.535
It's a set-- it's a
linear combination

00:52:29.535 --> 00:52:31.910
of these functions, which is
cosine, cosine 2t, and blah,

00:52:31.910 --> 00:52:33.200
blah, blah.

00:52:33.200 --> 00:52:35.810
I guess you could also call
it an even Fourier series

00:52:35.810 --> 00:52:37.160
or truncated--

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:39.560
lots of people-- you could
call it lots of things.

00:52:39.560 --> 00:52:40.400
Fine.

00:52:40.400 --> 00:52:45.320
OK, then my set S is the set
of coefficients for which this

00:52:45.320 --> 00:52:49.880
function is less than
1 as long-- for all t,

00:52:49.880 --> 00:52:52.340
absolute value t less than--

00:52:52.340 --> 00:52:54.410
this is symmetric.

00:52:54.410 --> 00:52:56.310
If I plug in minus t,
It's the same thing.

00:52:56.310 --> 00:53:00.560
So I just-- it's basically
t between 0 and pi over 3.

00:53:00.560 --> 00:53:02.390
So everybody got it?

00:53:02.390 --> 00:53:03.440
So that's it.

00:53:03.440 --> 00:53:04.730
That's my set.

00:53:04.730 --> 00:53:06.810
And look, if you were
in EE or something,

00:53:06.810 --> 00:53:08.820
you could say, oh, these
are the things that

00:53:08.820 --> 00:53:11.740
satisfy some filter that does
something, and blah, blah,

00:53:11.740 --> 00:53:12.240
blah.

00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:14.070
That's fine.

00:53:14.070 --> 00:53:19.950
OK, so the question
is it convex?

00:53:19.950 --> 00:53:20.610
It is.

00:53:20.610 --> 00:53:24.120
Otherwise, it wouldn't be as
an example in the lecture.

00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:26.070
But the question is,
how do you show that?

00:53:26.070 --> 00:53:30.150
And, in fact, it's not
too hard to actually

00:53:30.150 --> 00:53:33.150
just go brute force on this.

00:53:33.150 --> 00:53:35.830
And so to go brute force
what you do is you'd say,

00:53:35.830 --> 00:53:42.330
well, suppose I had two entries,
two members of this set.

00:53:42.330 --> 00:53:43.050
Here's one.

00:53:43.050 --> 00:53:46.320
And I'm going to draw,
not the coefficients,

00:53:46.320 --> 00:53:48.580
but I'll draw the actual
trigonometric polynomial.

00:53:48.580 --> 00:53:49.410
So here's one.

00:53:49.410 --> 00:53:52.740
And sure enough, look, it's
between plus minus 1 out

00:53:52.740 --> 00:53:53.550
to pi over 3.

00:53:53.550 --> 00:53:54.690
So here's one.

00:53:54.690 --> 00:53:58.080
And here's another
one, like that.

00:53:58.080 --> 00:54:01.650
Then what I would do in my head,
and you can just start with

00:54:01.650 --> 00:54:04.020
the average because if it
doesn't hold for the average--

00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:05.970
anyway, that's-- so
then you ask yourself,

00:54:05.970 --> 00:54:08.907
if I form the average of
those two-- now technically,

00:54:08.907 --> 00:54:10.740
I'm forming the average
of the coefficients.

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:12.270
But when I form the average
of the coefficients,

00:54:12.270 --> 00:54:13.200
I get the average.

00:54:13.200 --> 00:54:16.080
I literally just average
these two functions.

00:54:16.080 --> 00:54:18.780
And I get this dotted
curve here like that.

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:21.190
And then we ask
ourselves the question

00:54:21.190 --> 00:54:23.910
if a function is
between plus and minus 1

00:54:23.910 --> 00:54:27.390
over this little boundary,
and so is another one,

00:54:27.390 --> 00:54:30.090
and then I form the average
of those two functions,

00:54:30.090 --> 00:54:31.830
is it between plus
and minus 1 over that?

00:54:31.830 --> 00:54:32.700
The answer is, yeah.

00:54:32.700 --> 00:54:33.540
Of course, it is.

00:54:33.540 --> 00:54:35.640
Yeah, so it's convex.

00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:39.300
There's a derivation of it.

00:54:39.300 --> 00:54:41.520
But we can also think
it's a really cool way

00:54:41.520 --> 00:54:44.980
to think of S as an
infinite intersection.

00:54:44.980 --> 00:54:50.160
So I'm going to write that
down because it's actually

00:54:50.160 --> 00:54:51.700
interesting.

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:54.402
It goes like this.

00:54:54.402 --> 00:54:58.710
It's the set of x for
which, and then it's here.

00:54:58.710 --> 00:55:08.400
I'll just write it is less
than 1 for t in 0 pi over 3.

00:55:08.400 --> 00:55:10.920
So it looks like that.

00:55:10.920 --> 00:55:13.900
So I'm going to
ask you a question.

00:55:13.900 --> 00:55:16.320
Let's say I need a number
between 0 and pi over 3.

00:55:16.320 --> 00:55:18.780
Here it is, 0.6.

00:55:18.780 --> 00:55:22.780
So I would like you to tell
me what does this look like?

00:55:22.780 --> 00:55:24.960
What kind of-- in fact,
describe this geometrically?

00:55:24.960 --> 00:55:27.490
We haven't given
a name for it yet.

00:55:27.490 --> 00:55:28.470
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!

00:55:28.470 --> 00:55:29.550
Very wrong.

00:55:29.550 --> 00:55:30.390
There you go.

00:55:30.390 --> 00:55:36.030
And this is for t
equals 0.6 there.

00:55:36.030 --> 00:55:38.360
Everybody got that?

00:55:38.360 --> 00:55:43.190
So the question is, what
does this look like?

00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:49.050
A subset.

00:55:49.050 --> 00:55:49.860
Is it what?

00:55:49.860 --> 00:55:51.210
A subset.

00:55:51.210 --> 00:55:52.110
Which set?

00:55:52.110 --> 00:55:54.480
A subset, the second one.

00:55:54.480 --> 00:55:57.080
Is it a subset?

00:55:57.080 --> 00:55:58.530
Halfspace.

00:55:58.530 --> 00:56:00.100
It's a halfspace?

00:56:00.100 --> 00:56:01.870
Is that what-- yeah.

00:56:01.870 --> 00:56:02.610
Yeah.

00:56:02.610 --> 00:56:10.560
OK, so actually, this,
that's a halfspace in x.

00:56:10.560 --> 00:56:14.430
So in other words, the
set of coefficients

00:56:14.430 --> 00:56:18.120
of trigonometric polynomials
so that it evaluated at 0.6

00:56:18.120 --> 00:56:18.870
is less than 1.

00:56:18.870 --> 00:56:20.820
That's a halfspace
in coefficient space.

00:56:20.820 --> 00:56:21.540
You know what?

00:56:21.540 --> 00:56:24.060
So is this one.

00:56:24.060 --> 00:56:25.615
That's a halfspace.

00:56:25.615 --> 00:56:27.240
So it's the intersection
of halfspaces.

00:56:27.240 --> 00:56:28.073
That's a polyhedron.

00:56:28.073 --> 00:56:31.140
As a matter of fact, the two
halfspaces are just shifted.

00:56:31.140 --> 00:56:34.410
And if I ask you what's
the set of x for which,

00:56:34.410 --> 00:56:38.370
let's say, a transpose x--

00:56:38.370 --> 00:56:39.270
yeah, that's fine.

00:56:39.270 --> 00:56:42.600
I'll just do this.
--is less than b--

00:56:42.600 --> 00:56:44.190
note the absolute value here.

00:56:44.190 --> 00:56:46.350
That's actually a
really cool thing.

00:56:46.350 --> 00:56:47.940
It looks like this.

00:56:47.940 --> 00:56:49.740
It's actually a slab.

00:56:49.740 --> 00:56:53.295
Here's a, and here's minus a.

00:56:53.295 --> 00:56:54.990
You can check me on that.

00:56:54.990 --> 00:56:57.705
So a really good name
for this is it's a slab.

00:57:00.780 --> 00:57:02.130
And it's convex.

00:57:02.130 --> 00:57:04.140
It's the intersection
of halfspaces.

00:57:04.140 --> 00:57:05.460
So it's convex.

00:57:05.460 --> 00:57:07.900
And so I'm going to
think of this thing

00:57:07.900 --> 00:57:13.570
as this thing is the
intersection overall t in 0

00:57:13.570 --> 00:57:16.360
pi over 3 of--

00:57:16.360 --> 00:57:17.710
I'm going to call this that.

00:57:17.710 --> 00:57:18.910
I'm going to call it slab--

00:57:18.910 --> 00:57:21.140
S sub t is going to be the slab.

00:57:21.140 --> 00:57:25.060
So this set, whatever it is,
it's an intersection of slabs.

00:57:25.060 --> 00:57:27.100
But it's an infinite
intersection of slabs.

00:57:27.100 --> 00:57:29.470
There is one for
every value of t.

00:57:29.470 --> 00:57:32.140
Everybody got this, right?

00:57:32.140 --> 00:57:34.600
So over here, what
you can see is

00:57:34.600 --> 00:57:37.660
what these are, what
these lines here,

00:57:37.660 --> 00:57:41.710
is each one corresponds to
a t between 0 and pi over 3.

00:57:41.710 --> 00:57:44.890
And they're going to be
two parallel lines here,

00:57:44.890 --> 00:57:46.210
two parallel lines.

00:57:46.210 --> 00:57:49.240
And basically everything
in between that slab

00:57:49.240 --> 00:57:53.350
those are the points for which
that trigonometric polynomial,

00:57:53.350 --> 00:57:57.310
at that point t, is less
than 1 in absolute value.

00:57:57.310 --> 00:57:58.340
Everybody got that?

00:57:58.340 --> 00:58:01.330
And so basically, if you're
in all of these, you're cool.

00:58:01.330 --> 00:58:02.890
And you can see
actually it's curved.

00:58:02.890 --> 00:58:05.620
The set is curved and
weird stuff, but that's it.

00:58:05.620 --> 00:58:07.795
So this make sense?

00:58:12.200 --> 00:58:15.060
Now we get to affine functions.

00:58:15.060 --> 00:58:19.910
So an affine function is
basically a linear function

00:58:19.910 --> 00:58:21.260
plus a constant.

00:58:21.260 --> 00:58:24.380
By the way, there's a lot
of confusion about that.

00:58:24.380 --> 00:58:28.850
And people talk about a linear
model and a linear function

00:58:28.850 --> 00:58:30.140
when they mean affine.

00:58:30.140 --> 00:58:30.848
And I don't mind.

00:58:30.848 --> 00:58:33.057
It doesn't bother me as long
as I know that they know

00:58:33.057 --> 00:58:34.200
what they're talking about.

00:58:34.200 --> 00:58:36.800
But it does bother me when
I know that they don't know

00:58:36.800 --> 00:58:37.970
what they're talking about.

00:58:37.970 --> 00:58:43.280
So you're allowed to
confuse affine and linear

00:58:43.280 --> 00:58:46.910
only if you absolutely for
sure know the difference.

00:58:46.910 --> 00:58:48.840
So for a while,
I'll try to be good.

00:58:48.840 --> 00:58:51.860
And I'll just say affine
when I mean affine.

00:58:51.860 --> 00:58:55.890
So it would be like you'd say
piecewise linear function,

00:58:55.890 --> 00:58:56.685
which--

00:58:56.685 --> 00:58:57.560
you know really mean.

00:58:57.560 --> 00:58:59.840
You really mean
piecewise affine.

00:58:59.840 --> 00:59:01.130
But it sounds weird.

00:59:01.130 --> 00:59:02.840
People call it piecewise linear.

00:59:02.840 --> 00:59:04.820
And you sound kind
of a jerk if you

00:59:04.820 --> 00:59:08.360
say things like, oh, I'm sorry,
did you mean piecewise affine?

00:59:08.360 --> 00:59:11.960
And they're like,
whatever, dude.

00:59:11.960 --> 00:59:13.700
So that's-- OK, fine.

00:59:13.700 --> 00:59:15.710
So that's an affine function.

00:59:15.710 --> 00:59:20.420
OK, so affine functions
preserve convex sets.

00:59:20.420 --> 00:59:23.450
Basically going forward,
that's called the image,

00:59:23.450 --> 00:59:25.120
and actually pulling back.

00:59:25.120 --> 00:59:26.370
It's called the inverse image.

00:59:26.370 --> 00:59:31.820
So what it says is the image--

00:59:31.820 --> 00:59:37.520
if you have a convex set and I
apply an affine function to it,

00:59:37.520 --> 00:59:38.180
then--

00:59:38.180 --> 00:59:40.640
and I look at--
which means I apply

00:59:40.640 --> 00:59:43.280
the affine function,
every element in the set,

00:59:43.280 --> 00:59:46.520
then that set is
going to be convex.

00:59:46.520 --> 00:59:48.770
And these are just
straightforward to show,

00:59:48.770 --> 00:59:50.300
so is the inverse image.

00:59:50.300 --> 00:59:53.270
Now, here I have to talk
about this for a minute.

00:59:53.270 --> 00:59:56.030
Everybody knows F is a
function, and F inverse

00:59:56.030 --> 01:00:00.030
has a definition in it.

01:00:00.030 --> 01:00:02.210
So the standard
definition is that that's

01:00:02.210 --> 01:00:03.510
the inverse function.

01:00:03.510 --> 01:00:05.210
And so you wouldn't
write f inverse

01:00:05.210 --> 01:00:07.340
unless f were convertible.

01:00:07.340 --> 01:00:10.940
But there's another
completely standard use

01:00:10.940 --> 01:00:16.140
in mathematics of F inverse, and
it is not a function for sure.

01:00:16.140 --> 01:00:17.340
It means this.

01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:18.930
It's called the inverse image.

01:00:18.930 --> 01:00:23.700
It's the way you say
it, and it's the set.

01:00:23.700 --> 01:00:27.240
F inverse of a set is all of
the things that get mapped

01:00:27.240 --> 01:00:31.200
into C. So that's
the inverse image,

01:00:31.200 --> 01:00:35.460
and it's absolutely
completely standard.

01:00:35.460 --> 01:00:40.080
Do not confuse-- by the way,
if F is an invertible function,

01:00:40.080 --> 01:00:43.980
then there's a connection
between this notation

01:00:43.980 --> 01:00:45.670
and the other one.

01:00:45.670 --> 01:00:46.890
But this is the absolute.

01:00:46.890 --> 01:00:48.810
This is a standard worldwide.

01:00:48.810 --> 01:00:50.910
Anybody using math
would know this.

01:00:50.910 --> 01:00:52.290
OK, so that's the inverse image.

01:00:52.290 --> 01:00:56.850
Both of these operations
preserve convexity.

01:00:56.850 --> 01:01:01.410
So here's an-- an
example that would be

01:01:01.410 --> 01:01:04.920
like some kind of projection.

01:01:04.920 --> 01:01:09.690
So I could go back
here, and I could say--

01:01:09.690 --> 01:01:10.890
here's a beautiful one.

01:01:10.890 --> 01:01:16.200
Let's have trigonometric
polynomials of degree 5

01:01:16.200 --> 01:01:18.090
that satisfy this.

01:01:18.090 --> 01:01:19.920
Here's my linear mapping.

01:01:19.920 --> 01:01:23.380
I will simply take the
first two coefficients.

01:01:23.380 --> 01:01:26.460
So I take x1 to x5,
and I return x1x2.

01:01:26.460 --> 01:01:29.100
That's obviously
a linear mapping.

01:01:29.100 --> 01:01:31.800
And now, I'll look
at the inverse image

01:01:31.800 --> 01:01:33.870
of that convex set
under that mapping,

01:01:33.870 --> 01:01:36.127
and then I'll say
precisely what it is.

01:01:36.127 --> 01:01:37.560
It is the set--

01:01:37.560 --> 01:01:40.410
and I'll make it sound
all fancy, but it's not.

01:01:40.410 --> 01:01:43.380
I would say it's the
set of all the first two

01:01:43.380 --> 01:01:46.560
coefficients of a
trigonometric polynomial that

01:01:46.560 --> 01:01:50.850
can be completed to a
trigonometric polynomial that

01:01:50.850 --> 01:01:52.860
satisfies this.

01:01:52.860 --> 01:01:54.090
Everybody get that?

01:01:54.090 --> 01:01:57.270
It's getting weird and esoteric
now, but still, don't worry.

01:01:57.270 --> 01:01:59.890
That's fine.

01:01:59.890 --> 01:02:04.600
So these are those
types of things.

01:02:04.600 --> 01:02:07.840
And now we're going to
get good to one weird one,

01:02:07.840 --> 01:02:10.780
and it's something you
didn't know and/or--

01:02:10.780 --> 01:02:12.970
sorry, you didn't know
and isn't obvious.

01:02:12.970 --> 01:02:13.960
And so this is it.

01:02:13.960 --> 01:02:16.120
Good, because I hate
the thought of having

01:02:16.120 --> 01:02:18.370
a whole lecture where
everything is kind of like, duh,

01:02:18.370 --> 01:02:21.190
or, well, I didn't say.

01:02:21.190 --> 01:02:22.450
It's just basic.

01:02:22.450 --> 01:02:23.620
OK, here it is.

01:02:23.620 --> 01:02:24.800
This is interesting.

01:02:24.800 --> 01:02:29.560
So the perspective
function takes a pair of--

01:02:29.560 --> 01:02:32.860
it takes an N plus 1
vector, which we break up

01:02:32.860 --> 01:02:35.890
into a vector, and n
vector, and a scalar, which

01:02:35.890 --> 01:02:38.320
we call x and t, and
it simply divides

01:02:38.320 --> 01:02:41.380
by the last one, last entry.

01:02:41.380 --> 01:02:47.380
OK, so by the way, if you
work in vision or some people

01:02:47.380 --> 01:02:50.110
do crazy affine--

01:02:50.110 --> 01:02:52.418
they do weird
transformations this way.

01:02:52.418 --> 01:02:52.960
I don't know.

01:02:52.960 --> 01:02:54.770
Some people who do
robotics do that anyway.

01:02:54.770 --> 01:02:58.810
So you might be
familiar with this.

01:02:58.810 --> 01:03:03.690
OK, so this is called
a perspective function.

01:03:03.690 --> 01:03:06.840
It is, of course, not
linear, not remotely.

01:03:06.840 --> 01:03:10.260
Well, if you fix t,
It's linear in x.

01:03:10.260 --> 01:03:13.320
But it is definitely
not linear in t.

01:03:13.320 --> 01:03:16.690
OK, so that's a
perspective function.

01:03:16.690 --> 01:03:20.610
And it turns out that images and
inverse images of convex sets

01:03:20.610 --> 01:03:25.220
are preserved under
perspective functions.

01:03:25.220 --> 01:03:31.340
And that is not obvious,
I think, remotely.

01:03:31.340 --> 01:03:33.810
So and here, here's
a good example.

01:03:33.810 --> 01:03:36.260
So if I combine this
with a linear mapping,

01:03:36.260 --> 01:03:38.310
I get a so-called linear
fractional function.

01:03:38.310 --> 01:03:39.560
So that's an affine--

01:03:39.560 --> 01:03:42.660
no, you should call it affine
fractional, but whatever.

01:03:42.660 --> 01:03:44.690
So here, that's an
affine function,

01:03:44.690 --> 01:03:50.260
and you divide by this,
a scalar affine function.

01:03:50.260 --> 01:03:52.010
This is called linear
fractional function.

01:03:52.010 --> 01:03:52.677
Looks like that.

01:03:52.677 --> 01:03:54.770
And by the way, our
rule here is the domain

01:03:54.770 --> 01:03:56.865
is this thing is
going to be positive.

01:03:56.865 --> 01:03:58.490
So that's a linear
fractional function.

01:03:58.490 --> 01:04:01.515
They come up in a
ton of applications.

01:04:01.515 --> 01:04:03.140
They come up--
actually, this is things

01:04:03.140 --> 01:04:07.820
like if you're conditioning
in probability.

01:04:07.820 --> 01:04:10.970
Conditioning is actually
nothing but a linear fractional

01:04:10.970 --> 01:04:14.960
function on, like a probability
mass function, for example.

01:04:14.960 --> 01:04:18.770
It comes up in
vision all the time.

01:04:18.770 --> 01:04:22.880
So for example, if I
say-- if I see a 3D thing,

01:04:22.880 --> 01:04:26.520
and I want to know what
is the image on my retina,

01:04:26.520 --> 01:04:28.980
let's pretend my
retina is flat--

01:04:28.980 --> 01:04:31.240
so if I say what is it?

01:04:31.240 --> 01:04:33.610
The answer is it's a
linear fractional function.

01:04:33.610 --> 01:04:35.010
That's what it is.

01:04:35.010 --> 01:04:39.540
And this because look,
if I hold this like this,

01:04:39.540 --> 01:04:44.880
and I look at it, you know
what's happening here.

01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:48.360
Let's make it-- this
is good enough here.

01:04:48.360 --> 01:04:50.790
Yeah, so obviously,
this is closer.

01:04:50.790 --> 01:04:51.930
So that's actually bigger.

01:04:51.930 --> 01:04:54.550
Now, our brains have
evolved to handle that,

01:04:54.550 --> 01:04:57.570
and that's a cue that
this is closer than that.

01:04:57.570 --> 01:04:59.880
But whatever the image
is like in a photograph

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:03.420
or on my retina, it's
definitely not a linear mapping.

01:05:03.420 --> 01:05:08.700
So that's a linear
fractional function.

01:05:08.700 --> 01:05:10.950
If you want to believe
this, it's you--

01:05:10.950 --> 01:05:12.450
it's in the book,
you could read it.

01:05:12.450 --> 01:05:14.550
It's whatever, half
a page to prove it.

01:05:14.550 --> 01:05:16.770
It's not hard.

01:05:16.770 --> 01:05:19.500
But a good way to
think of it intuitively

01:05:19.500 --> 01:05:23.160
is this is what I'll show you.

01:05:23.160 --> 01:05:25.400
I'll argue-- this is just
going to be completely

01:05:25.400 --> 01:05:26.400
intuitive and geometric.

01:05:26.400 --> 01:05:29.820
But I'm going to argue
that line segments get

01:05:29.820 --> 01:05:32.820
mapped to line segments.

01:05:32.820 --> 01:05:36.450
And that's good enough because
then if my function is convex,

01:05:36.450 --> 01:05:41.790
and I know that every line
segment that's in your set

01:05:41.790 --> 01:05:44.490
gets mapped to 1, then
that's good enough.

01:05:44.490 --> 01:05:47.200
So then the question is
this-- here's a line segment,

01:05:47.200 --> 01:05:49.110
and I look at it.

01:05:49.110 --> 01:05:51.810
So I look at my and, I
then we ask, what does it

01:05:51.810 --> 01:05:53.430
look like on my retina?

01:05:53.430 --> 01:05:56.130
The answer is it's
a line segment.

01:05:56.130 --> 01:05:57.600
Do we agree?

01:05:57.600 --> 01:05:59.222
--in two dimensions.

01:05:59.222 --> 01:06:00.930
Now, I'm going to ask
you a question just

01:06:00.930 --> 01:06:03.600
to make sure we're not
fooling ourselves here.

01:06:03.600 --> 01:06:07.170
The midpoint of this thing
maps to a point on my retina.

01:06:07.170 --> 01:06:10.650
Is it the midpoint of the
line segment on my retina?

01:06:10.650 --> 01:06:12.120
No, it is not.

01:06:12.120 --> 01:06:14.880
OK, so that tells
you you're not-- oh,

01:06:14.880 --> 01:06:17.280
by the way, unless
I'm way, way far away,

01:06:17.280 --> 01:06:21.210
and that's an approximation
if you have a telephoto

01:06:21.210 --> 01:06:24.660
lens, that image
mapping, image formation

01:06:24.660 --> 01:06:29.460
is pretty much linear, but
not if you're up close.

01:06:29.460 --> 01:06:32.070
That's my visual argument
that uses a pencil

01:06:32.070 --> 01:06:37.980
to argue that perspective
mappings preserve convexity.

01:06:37.980 --> 01:06:43.040
OK, here's just a quick example
to see what this looks like.

01:06:43.040 --> 01:06:46.280
So here's a linear
fractional function.

01:06:46.280 --> 01:06:51.020
It's x divided by-- you just
scale x wherever you are.

01:06:51.020 --> 01:06:54.150
How you scale it depends
on this denominator,

01:06:54.150 --> 01:06:56.030
which is x1 plus x2 plus 1.

01:06:56.030 --> 01:06:57.140
We assume that's positive.

01:06:57.140 --> 01:06:58.682
So that says that
you live over here.

01:06:58.682 --> 01:07:00.080
This is like the netherworld.

01:07:00.080 --> 01:07:01.417
We don't go there.

01:07:01.417 --> 01:07:02.750
But this is where it's positive.

01:07:02.750 --> 01:07:07.828
And this coefficient here,
right over here, is gigantic.

01:07:07.828 --> 01:07:09.620
And it gets smaller
and smaller and smaller

01:07:09.620 --> 01:07:10.980
the farther you go away.

01:07:10.980 --> 01:07:16.880
So if this is the set C, this
is the image under f of C,

01:07:16.880 --> 01:07:20.840
and it's this bump right here
that translates over to there.

01:07:20.840 --> 01:07:24.320
And a good way to understand
this is take this thing,

01:07:24.320 --> 01:07:28.160
put it on the ground like that,
and now fly a drone over it

01:07:28.160 --> 01:07:32.300
and look down, and
you get close enough.

01:07:32.300 --> 01:07:34.610
I think the word is
it's foreshortening.

01:07:34.610 --> 01:07:36.200
So you get close enough.

01:07:36.200 --> 01:07:38.240
Then, in fact, this
thing is closer to you.

01:07:38.240 --> 01:07:39.450
So it looks all big.

01:07:39.450 --> 01:07:41.783
It looks like-- and that's
what it's going to look like.

01:07:41.783 --> 01:07:44.820
So that's why these things
look very familiar to us

01:07:44.820 --> 01:07:48.000
because, well, hey,
it's how our eyes work.

01:07:48.000 --> 01:07:50.520
So everybody got this?

01:07:50.520 --> 01:07:55.740
And it is not remotely obvious
that these preserve convexity.

01:07:55.740 --> 01:07:59.670
But they do, and we'll see
some crazy consequences

01:07:59.670 --> 01:08:03.852
of this later in various
practical settings.

01:08:03.852 --> 01:08:05.310
It's going to be
super interesting.

01:08:08.150 --> 01:08:13.440
Next topic is
generalized inequalities.

01:08:13.440 --> 01:08:15.095
So we've actually
already seen two,

01:08:15.095 --> 01:08:17.220
and we've already seen the
two most important ones.

01:08:17.220 --> 01:08:19.590
So let me explain what it is.

01:08:19.590 --> 01:08:22.790
So suppose I have a convex cone.

01:08:22.790 --> 01:08:26.149
Now, what that means-- it's
a proper cone if it's closed.

01:08:26.149 --> 01:08:28.790
So it contains
limits or whatever.

01:08:28.790 --> 01:08:32.370
Again, that's analysis, and we
don't have to worry about it.

01:08:32.370 --> 01:08:33.260
It's solid.

01:08:33.260 --> 01:08:35.420
That's a technical
term that means

01:08:35.420 --> 01:08:36.619
it has a nonempty interior.

01:08:39.500 --> 01:08:42.319
And it's pointed, which
means it contains no line.

01:08:42.319 --> 01:08:44.330
Again, I think these
are really good.

01:08:44.330 --> 01:08:47.420
I don't know who chose these
words, but excellent choice,

01:08:47.420 --> 01:08:51.740
I think, because they're
completely evocative.

01:08:51.740 --> 01:08:54.410
OK, so here's an example,
is the nonnegative orthant.

01:08:54.410 --> 01:08:55.910
That's a famous one.

01:08:55.910 --> 01:08:57.439
It's going to come up a lot.

01:08:57.439 --> 01:09:00.800
nonnegative orthant, it looks
like it's kind of a weird name.

01:09:00.800 --> 01:09:03.439
In 2D, it's just this thing.

01:09:03.439 --> 01:09:07.470
In 3D, there's eight orthans
and it's one of them.

01:09:07.470 --> 01:09:09.420
It's orthant number one or--

01:09:09.420 --> 01:09:12.361
I don't know how you I don't
know how people name those.

01:09:12.361 --> 01:09:14.069
In fact, I didn't even
know what this is.

01:09:14.069 --> 01:09:16.950
How do people-- is this
quadrant 2 or quadrant--

01:09:16.950 --> 01:09:20.850
anyway, whatever your
quadrant labeling method is,

01:09:20.850 --> 01:09:22.710
this is one is a quadrant.

01:09:22.710 --> 01:09:25.470
So why you would call it
orthant in higher dimensions?

01:09:25.470 --> 01:09:28.380
I don't know, but
it's what's stuck.

01:09:28.380 --> 01:09:32.040
So language is weird,
and it's just-- so it's

01:09:32.040 --> 01:09:34.470
a set of nonnegative vectors.

01:09:34.470 --> 01:09:37.359
Positive semidefinite
cone, we already saw that.

01:09:37.359 --> 01:09:40.260
But it turns out it's closed.

01:09:40.260 --> 01:09:41.790
And it's solid.

01:09:41.790 --> 01:09:43.649
In other words,
there is no matrix

01:09:43.649 --> 01:09:47.250
that is positive
semidefinite, and its negative

01:09:47.250 --> 01:09:49.920
is positive semi-definite
except zero.

01:09:49.920 --> 01:09:51.450
That's the only one.

01:09:51.450 --> 01:09:55.020
So that's what it
means to be pointed.

01:09:55.020 --> 01:09:56.880
And you get weird
stuff like that,

01:09:56.880 --> 01:09:59.400
the set of coefficients
of polynomials that are

01:09:59.400 --> 01:10:01.650
nonnegative on some interval.

01:10:01.650 --> 01:10:04.650
I'm just mentioning this because
there's some just weird ones.

01:10:04.650 --> 01:10:06.510
So these are called--

01:10:06.510 --> 01:10:09.660
these are going to be
called proper cones.

01:10:09.660 --> 01:10:10.920
And let me explain.

01:10:10.920 --> 01:10:14.310
Let me show you some improper
cones in R2 to make it clear.

01:10:17.760 --> 01:10:20.550
So oh, here's a cone.

01:10:20.550 --> 01:10:22.320
By the way, this is
just called a ray.

01:10:22.320 --> 01:10:25.110
So it's all points that go--

01:10:25.110 --> 01:10:27.570
that are multiples
of this vector

01:10:27.570 --> 01:10:28.890
and just go off that way.

01:10:28.890 --> 01:10:30.870
That's called a ray.

01:10:30.870 --> 01:10:31.830
It's a cone.

01:10:31.830 --> 01:10:34.470
It's a convex cone.

01:10:34.470 --> 01:10:35.490
Where does it fail?

01:10:35.490 --> 01:10:37.150
What does it fail there?

01:10:37.150 --> 01:10:39.350
What does it violate?

01:10:39.350 --> 01:10:40.110
It's not solid.

01:10:40.110 --> 01:10:41.250
It's not solid.

01:10:41.250 --> 01:10:41.940
That's right.

01:10:41.940 --> 01:10:47.010
So solid means it's got
to have some interior.

01:10:47.010 --> 01:10:48.120
Here's a cone.

01:10:48.120 --> 01:10:49.530
It's a halfspace.

01:10:49.530 --> 01:10:52.080
That's totally a cone in R2.

01:10:52.080 --> 01:10:54.240
How does it fail?

01:10:54.240 --> 01:10:55.290
It's not pointed.

01:10:55.290 --> 01:10:55.920
Why?

01:10:55.920 --> 01:10:57.430
Because look at this.

01:10:57.430 --> 01:10:58.770
Here's a point.

01:10:58.770 --> 01:11:01.410
Here's a line that is
contained in that cone.

01:11:01.410 --> 01:11:07.230
And that's that violates
the idea that it's pointed.

01:11:07.230 --> 01:11:08.940
So how about this?

01:11:08.940 --> 01:11:09.780
How about this cone?

01:11:14.990 --> 01:11:16.080
How about that one?

01:11:20.340 --> 01:11:22.140
That's good.

01:11:22.140 --> 01:11:25.860
That pointed, solid,
whatever-- it's a cone.

01:11:25.860 --> 01:11:26.910
OK, all right.

01:11:26.910 --> 01:11:29.160
Fine.

01:11:29.160 --> 01:11:35.340
OK, so we will use
proper cones to define

01:11:35.340 --> 01:11:39.210
a generalized inequality
among vectors.

01:11:39.210 --> 01:11:43.020
And so the way you
do that is we'll

01:11:43.020 --> 01:11:46.110
just say when you
say X is less than Y,

01:11:46.110 --> 01:11:47.160
you would pronounce this.

01:11:47.160 --> 01:11:48.660
If you were being
super fancy, you'd

01:11:48.660 --> 01:11:50.790
say X is less than
Y with respect

01:11:50.790 --> 01:11:53.880
to the cone K. That'd
be how you'd say it.

01:11:53.880 --> 01:11:58.020
But all it means is that
Y minus x is in the cone.

01:11:58.020 --> 01:12:00.120
And look, you already do this.

01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:03.780
If you say one-- if you
know one matrix is--

01:12:03.780 --> 01:12:08.190
one symmetric matrix is bigger
than another, if you build an--

01:12:08.190 --> 01:12:09.898
if this is your
estimation covariance,

01:12:09.898 --> 01:12:11.940
and this is someone else's
estimation covariance,

01:12:11.940 --> 01:12:15.870
and yours is bigger
than theirs, then theirs

01:12:15.870 --> 01:12:17.650
is better than yours.

01:12:17.650 --> 01:12:19.990
But this has no judgment here.

01:12:19.990 --> 01:12:21.650
So that's what that means.

01:12:21.650 --> 01:12:26.440
So OK, now, theres lots
of things you can--

01:12:26.440 --> 01:12:29.380
if you do this with the
cone, the nonnegative

01:12:29.380 --> 01:12:32.260
orthant this inequality
turns into just ordinary

01:12:32.260 --> 01:12:33.580
element-wise inequality.

01:12:33.580 --> 01:12:37.630
And by the way, most people
actually write that these days.

01:12:37.630 --> 01:12:39.760
You just write it this way.

01:12:39.760 --> 01:12:42.790
It's polite for the next 10,
15, 20 years before the language

01:12:42.790 --> 01:12:46.090
converges to, at first,
use of this to say

01:12:46.090 --> 01:12:48.970
where the inequality
means element-wise.

01:12:48.970 --> 01:12:52.810
OK, but we'll use
the squiggly here.

01:12:52.810 --> 01:12:53.650
I don't know why.

01:12:53.650 --> 01:12:55.698
I don't know how we're
going to change the book.

01:12:55.698 --> 01:12:57.490
I'd probably not use
the squiggly for that.

01:12:57.490 --> 01:12:58.480
But it doesn't matter.

01:12:58.480 --> 01:13:00.370
OK.

01:13:00.370 --> 01:13:03.940
And the same is true
for matrix inequality,

01:13:03.940 --> 01:13:07.750
that these are so common
that we drop this,

01:13:07.750 --> 01:13:09.750
and just-- so basically,
from now on, you

01:13:09.750 --> 01:13:11.500
see a squiggly inequality
between vectors.

01:13:11.500 --> 01:13:15.190
It means element-wise, you see a
squiggly inequality between two

01:13:15.190 --> 01:13:17.760
symmetric matrices, it means--

01:13:17.760 --> 01:13:20.260
some people call it what the
loner is ordering or something.

01:13:20.260 --> 01:13:21.700
That's a very fancy
way to say it.

01:13:21.700 --> 01:13:25.240
It means the difference
is positive semi-definite.

01:13:25.240 --> 01:13:26.920
That's what it means.

01:13:26.920 --> 01:13:29.150
So that's how that works.

01:13:29.150 --> 01:13:32.560
Now it turns out
when you're making

01:13:32.560 --> 01:13:37.540
a notation that's an inequality,
whole point of making notation

01:13:37.540 --> 01:13:38.470
is--

01:13:38.470 --> 01:13:43.480
so the idea of notation is
you want it to evoke something

01:13:43.480 --> 01:13:46.450
that you already know
about, everybody here

01:13:46.450 --> 01:13:49.690
knows about inequality between
numbers, everybody here.

01:13:49.690 --> 01:13:52.670
So it's like a norm,
an absolute value.

01:13:52.670 --> 01:13:55.270
You want-- the
notation is close.

01:13:55.270 --> 01:13:57.610
It's supposed to evoke that.

01:13:57.610 --> 01:14:00.620
Now it can't be just
that, or then it's silly.

01:14:00.620 --> 01:14:03.040
It's not a generalization.

01:14:03.040 --> 01:14:05.170
But the difference--
so what you really

01:14:05.170 --> 01:14:08.650
want is you want whenever you
see something like this, where

01:14:08.650 --> 01:14:13.240
we're going to overload or
extend some symbol in a more

01:14:13.240 --> 01:14:17.260
sophisticated setting,
you're supposed

01:14:17.260 --> 01:14:19.330
to import your intuition.

01:14:19.330 --> 01:14:22.053
So you'd say, yeah,
well, A is less than B,

01:14:22.053 --> 01:14:23.470
and C is less than
D, then I think

01:14:23.470 --> 01:14:26.800
a plus should be less than C
plus D. Most of those things

01:14:26.800 --> 01:14:30.400
you think are true,
and some are false.

01:14:30.400 --> 01:14:33.980
So you got to be on your
toes to know which is correct

01:14:33.980 --> 01:14:36.070
and which is not OK,
and that's your job.

01:14:36.070 --> 01:14:38.410
But that's what good
notation is for.

01:14:38.410 --> 01:14:40.318
That's like think
about matrices.

01:14:40.318 --> 01:14:41.110
What are you doing?

01:14:41.110 --> 01:14:42.040
You say I'm just--

01:14:42.040 --> 01:14:44.373
seriously, you're just you're
multiplying three numbers.

01:14:44.373 --> 01:14:45.490
And you're like, no, dude.

01:14:45.490 --> 01:14:48.472
That's a 10,000 by 1,000
matrix, that's a vector, that's

01:14:48.472 --> 01:14:49.180
blah, blah, blah.

01:14:49.180 --> 01:14:50.500
Do you see what I'm saying?

01:14:50.500 --> 01:14:53.380
But it looks the notation
looks super innocent.

01:14:53.380 --> 01:14:56.695
So that's the idea behind
good notation design.

01:15:00.070 --> 01:15:01.270
OK, so the main--

01:15:01.270 --> 01:15:03.280
what is the main
difference with vectors?

01:15:03.280 --> 01:15:05.590
Well, it's kind of obvious.

01:15:05.590 --> 01:15:10.900
If your base model for what
inequality is inequality on R,

01:15:10.900 --> 01:15:13.940
then that's got a property
which is extremely important.

01:15:13.940 --> 01:15:15.650
It's called the total ordering.

01:15:15.650 --> 01:15:17.650
And that means that
for any two numbers,

01:15:17.650 --> 01:15:20.020
either 1 is less than the
other or the other way around.

01:15:20.020 --> 01:15:21.100
Period.

01:15:21.100 --> 01:15:23.110
That's what it means
to be a linear ordering

01:15:23.110 --> 01:15:24.160
or a total ordering.

01:15:24.160 --> 01:15:27.350
Those would be the fancy
names in math, you would say.

01:15:27.350 --> 01:15:32.170
Now, for vectors, that's
just not the case.

01:15:32.170 --> 01:15:35.140
So that's interesting.

01:15:35.140 --> 01:15:39.285
So for example-- let's just
do a couple of examples.

01:15:42.310 --> 01:15:45.760
Let's look at the inequality
that looks like this.

01:15:50.950 --> 01:15:54.790
This says that two vector is
less than or equal to that one.

01:15:54.790 --> 01:15:57.790
What it means is that x is less
than u and y is less than v.

01:15:57.790 --> 01:16:01.450
So it's extremely easy
to find two points

01:16:01.450 --> 01:16:05.740
where neither two
vectors where it is false

01:16:05.740 --> 01:16:08.155
that one is less than the
other and the other way around.

01:16:08.155 --> 01:16:09.280
It's not a linear ordering.

01:16:09.280 --> 01:16:10.480
This is kind of obvious.

01:16:10.480 --> 01:16:13.900
And this is going to come up
later when, for example, these

01:16:13.900 --> 01:16:17.790
become multiple objectives
that we want small.

01:16:21.300 --> 01:16:23.640
I want to have a good fit on--

01:16:23.640 --> 01:16:25.870
I want to have good
training data fit,

01:16:25.870 --> 01:16:29.280
and I also want the complexity
of the model to be low.

01:16:29.280 --> 01:16:31.540
Then there are just going
to be two different things,

01:16:31.540 --> 01:16:32.998
and then you're
going to have-- you

01:16:32.998 --> 01:16:37.373
will have incomparable designs.

01:16:37.373 --> 01:16:39.790
It will neither be true that
one is bigger than the other.

01:16:39.790 --> 01:16:43.470
So what happens
now is you have--

01:16:43.470 --> 01:16:45.360
from your intuition
about numbers,

01:16:45.360 --> 01:16:49.200
you have ideas
involving minimum.

01:16:49.200 --> 01:16:51.630
You can talk about the
minimum of a bunch of numbers.

01:16:51.630 --> 01:16:52.260
Guess what?

01:16:52.260 --> 01:16:53.700
You cannot talk
about the minimum,

01:16:53.700 --> 01:16:55.560
but you can talk about the
minimum of a set of vectors,

01:16:55.560 --> 01:16:56.852
but it's a very specific thing.

01:16:56.852 --> 01:16:58.950
What happens is,
because it's not

01:16:58.950 --> 01:17:04.470
a linear ordering, a bunch of
ideas that were the same on R,

01:17:04.470 --> 01:17:06.000
they bifurcate.

01:17:06.000 --> 01:17:10.110
And now it turns out that all
your intuition about minimum,

01:17:10.110 --> 01:17:12.780
all this kind of stuff
in R, now there's

01:17:12.780 --> 01:17:14.790
going to be two concepts,
and actually they

01:17:14.790 --> 01:17:17.760
are minimum and minimal.

01:17:17.760 --> 01:17:19.450
So this is the picture.

01:17:19.450 --> 01:17:22.030
And again, this is weird
and abstract right now.

01:17:22.030 --> 01:17:26.650
All of this is going to have
a lot of applications later.

01:17:26.650 --> 01:17:28.480
So here's the definition.

01:17:28.480 --> 01:17:30.310
Minimum is super strong.

01:17:30.310 --> 01:17:34.870
It basically says this is
a minimum element, means

01:17:34.870 --> 01:17:38.710
everything else in the set is
comparable to me and bigger.

01:17:38.710 --> 01:17:41.110
Period.

01:17:41.110 --> 01:17:45.880
So for example, in the
minimum the minimum element

01:17:45.880 --> 01:17:50.410
of a set in, let's
say, in R2 with respect

01:17:50.410 --> 01:17:53.380
to the second order cone,
this is a perfect example.

01:17:53.380 --> 01:17:58.540
S1 has a minimum point,
and it's this one

01:17:58.540 --> 01:18:03.850
because everything in this set
is comparable to this point

01:18:03.850 --> 01:18:08.280
and bigger in the vector sense.

01:18:08.280 --> 01:18:09.780
So that's minimal.

01:18:09.780 --> 01:18:11.082
And it's easy to see.

01:18:11.082 --> 01:18:13.290
You can have a set that
doesn't have a minimal point.

01:18:13.290 --> 01:18:16.510
There's just-- sorry, minimum.

01:18:16.510 --> 01:18:17.010
Sorry.

01:18:17.010 --> 01:18:17.782
OK, minimum point.

01:18:17.782 --> 01:18:18.990
Doesn't have a minimum point.

01:18:18.990 --> 01:18:22.740
OK, now minimal
is a weaker term.

01:18:22.740 --> 01:18:27.540
And it says this,
a point is minimal

01:18:27.540 --> 01:18:31.630
if any point which is in the
set and better than or equal to,

01:18:31.630 --> 01:18:34.670
or less than or equal
to u, then it's u.

01:18:34.670 --> 01:18:38.430
So no one-- there's
no point in the set

01:18:38.430 --> 01:18:43.200
that is less than or
equal to u except u there.

01:18:43.200 --> 01:18:45.150
That's the right way to say it.

01:18:45.150 --> 01:18:46.410
And so here's a picture here.

01:18:46.410 --> 01:18:48.695
And geometrically,
what it says is here,

01:18:48.695 --> 01:18:50.070
you're saying that
every point is

01:18:50.070 --> 01:18:55.410
comparable to it, this orthant
or shifted orthant, that's

01:18:55.410 --> 01:18:58.170
all the points that
are comparable to x1

01:18:58.170 --> 01:18:59.520
and bigger than or equal to it.

01:18:59.520 --> 01:19:00.310
That's this thing.

01:19:00.310 --> 01:19:02.250
And sure enough, every
single point in the set

01:19:02.250 --> 01:19:04.140
is inside that set.

01:19:04.140 --> 01:19:07.840
Over here, this says that if
you look at the points which

01:19:07.840 --> 01:19:11.290
are less than or equal to
u, that's this down here,

01:19:11.290 --> 01:19:13.330
these two orphans
are the set of points

01:19:13.330 --> 01:19:16.180
that are incomparable to u.

01:19:16.180 --> 01:19:17.980
So this says you're comparable.

01:19:17.980 --> 01:19:22.840
If you're comparable and
less than or equal to u, then

01:19:22.840 --> 01:19:24.490
the only way-- and
you're in the set,

01:19:24.490 --> 01:19:26.260
the only point is
this intersection.

01:19:26.260 --> 01:19:27.760
It can be that.

01:19:27.760 --> 01:19:29.320
So this has got lots of names.

01:19:29.320 --> 01:19:31.240
It comes up in a
gazillion fields.

01:19:31.240 --> 01:19:33.130
We'll see.

01:19:33.130 --> 01:19:35.927
Sometimes, these are called,
depending on the context,

01:19:35.927 --> 01:19:37.510
they're going to be
called like Pareto

01:19:37.510 --> 01:19:40.090
points or efficient designs
or something like that.

01:19:40.090 --> 01:19:40.600
Yes.

01:19:40.600 --> 01:19:44.530
How do we know [INAUDIBLE] the
set of all comparable points?

01:19:44.530 --> 01:19:45.430
Which one?

01:19:45.430 --> 01:19:46.750
You said that [INAUDIBLE]

01:19:46.750 --> 01:19:47.510
Down here?

01:19:47.510 --> 01:19:48.010
Yeah.

01:19:48.010 --> 01:19:48.510
Yeah.

01:19:48.510 --> 01:19:49.900
Is it comparable--

01:19:49.900 --> 01:19:55.450
Oh, this is all the points that
are less than or equal to x2.

01:19:55.450 --> 01:19:56.560
You can check me on that.

01:19:59.280 --> 01:20:02.660
Well, comparable means
either the inequality

01:20:02.660 --> 01:20:06.110
works one way or the other.

01:20:06.110 --> 01:20:07.930
This is awesome,
but we should quit.

01:20:07.930 --> 01:20:09.520
We're actually
even a minute over.

01:20:09.520 --> 01:20:11.660
But it's fun.

01:20:11.660 --> 01:20:12.160
Cool.

01:20:12.160 --> 01:20:13.630
We'll quit here.

01:20:13.630 --> 01:20:17.700
And we'll continue
the slog next week.

